Say what the archive write is doing, and clear up after an interrupted one

Two faults with one cause: the longest step of a conversion reported nothing.

Extraction reports file N of 547 and finishes in well under a minute. Writing the
archive then takes minutes - a 15MB zip of several thousand entries onto console
storage - and said nothing at all. The dialog read "Done. Finishing up." with the bar
pinned at 100%, and the log was silent. That is indistinguishable from a hang, to a
player and to anyone reading a log afterwards, and it was taken for one repeatedly:
the write was killed four times on the assumption it had died, which is also how three
part-files came to be left behind.

libzip already measures this and only needed asking. The callback publishes a fraction
through `gOtrArchiveProgress`, which the extraction dialog shows on the same bar - the
write gets the bar to itself once extraction is done, labelled for what it is - and
logs it every ten percent so a run captured without a screen is still readable.

The second half is the mess. libzip writes to `<name>.XXXXXX.part` and renames it into
place when the archive closes, so a run interrupted during that write leaves the
temporary behind and nothing ever collects it. Two of them, 29MB between them, had
accumulated before anyone looked. `CreateArchive` now clears any temporary for the
target it is about to write, which is the moment they are known to be dead. Failures
there are ignored on purpose: not being able to tidy up is not a reason to refuse to
convert a ROM.

